{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe overestimation bias: The model consistently forecasts a slow, monotonic drift (up or down) while the true market exhibits high volatility with sharp spikes (e.g., +6-10 EUR) and rapid mean reversion. Apply a volatility multiplier or switch to a regime-switching model to capture sudden price jumps.",
    "Address the 'lag' in trend reversal: The model fails to anticipate the immediate peak and subsequent crash following a sentiment spike. When sentiment history shows consecutive high values (1.00) followed by a price spike, immediately reduce the forecast horizon by 15-20% to account for the inevitable correction.",
    "Invert the mean-reversion assumption: The model assumes prices will slowly converge to a long-term average. In reality, after a spike (e.g., >75 EUR), prices drop rapidly to the 68-70 EUR range. Force a steeper downward slope in the forecast immediately after any 3-day period where the price exceeds 74 EUR.",
    "Decouple sentiment from linear projection: High sentiment scores (1.00) in the history do not correlate with the model's linear upward drift; instead, they often precede a short-term rally followed by a sharp drop. Do not use sentiment as a direct linear driver for the 30-day forecast; use it only to trigger a binary 'spike' or 'crash' adjustment.",
    "Horizon-specific error correction: The model's error grows significantly beyond day 5. For forecasts beyond 10 days, anchor the prediction to the historical mean (approx. 70-71 EUR) rather than the model's projected trend line, as the model fails to capture the cyclical nature of the EU ETS market."
  ]
}
